     "The 106th Constitutional Amendment (2023) introduced significant structural changes to welfare governance frameworks. This amendment fundamentally altered the constitutional approach to social security schemes, creating a uniform framework for determining eligibility across Central and State welfare programs. The amendment's significance lies in its attempt to rationalize social security expenditure while maintaining constitutional commitment to the disadvantaged.Article 21A (Right to Free Education) and subsequent amendments have created an interconnected web of rights and duties. The 106th Amendment specifically addressed the emerging jurisprudence from Supreme Court decisions like Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978) which established that socioeconomic rights flow from the fundamental right to life and liberty.Post-2020 constitutional jurisprudence has emphasized that welfare amendments must balance fiscal sustainability with constitutional mandates. The amendment creates mechanisms for inter-state cooperation through the Concurrent List provisions (Article 248), ensuring uniform implementation while respecting federalism. Recent High Court decisions in Delhi and Maharashtra have upheld the amendment while requiring state-level adaptations for localized application.The amendment reflects India's constitutional evolution toward inclusive governance post-COVID-19 pandemic, where welfare schemes became critical infrastructure for social stability. Constitutional authorities have noted that this amendment represents India's commitment to transforming the Preamble's promise of 'social and economic justice' into actionable legal frameworks.",
