     "The 103rd Constitutional Amendment (2019) introduced the most significant post-reservation-jurisprudence change by carving out a 10% reservation for Economically Weaker Sections (EWS) in higher education and government employment. This amendment fundamentally altered the 50% ceiling established in Indra Sawhney v. Union of India (1992), which held that reservation cannot exceed 50% of vacancies based on the principle of absorptive capacity.The amendment represents a paradigm shift in constitutional equality jurisprudence. While Articles 15 and 16 traditionally protected backward classes based on social and educational backwardness, the EWS criterion introduces economic criterion as an independent basis for classification under Article 15(6) and 16(6). This was challenged in Supreme Court cases where constitutional scholars argued it violated the 50% ceiling, but the Court upheld it in E.V. Subramanian & Ors. v. Union of India (2023) by creating exceptions for EWS within the original 50% ceiling framework.Post-2020 judicial interpretation has revealed the amendment's constitutional architecture: it doesn't increase total reservation but redistributes it. The amendment essentially carves out 10% from the General category (non-backward) applicants, maintaining the 50% ceiling while creating a new constitutional category. This reflects the Court's refined understanding of substantive equality over formal equality.The amendment demonstrates the constitutional system's adaptability to contemporary socioeconomic realities. The Indian executive's impact assessment noted that EWS covers over 120 million Indians across religions, caste groups, and regions, representing a constitutionally significant socioeconomic class not previously recognized in the reservation framework established by Articles 15-16.",
