     "A",
     "The 42nd Constitutional Amendment (1976), enacted during the Emergency period, fundamentally restructured the constitutional framework by introducing environmental protection as a state duty. Article 48-A was inserted, making it a constitutional obligation for the State to 'endeavour to protect and improve the environment and to safeguard forests and wildlife of the country.' This amendment also modified the Preamble to include 'socialist' and 'secular', making it one of the most comprehensive amendments to the Constitution.The amendment was controversial for its substantive scope. Justice H.R. Khanna's dissent in ADM Jabalpur v. Shivakant Shukla (1976) regarding habeas corpus suspension shaped subsequent jurisprudence. The 44th Amendment (1978) partially restored constitutional balance by restricting emergency powers, but the environmental provisions of the 42nd Amendment remained foundational. The Supreme Court in Subhash Kumar v. State of Bihar (1991) derived the right to clean environment from Article 21, directly linking the amendment's directives to fundamental rights.The 42nd Amendment's environmental framework intersects with international environmental law. India's commitment to the UN Convention on Biodiversity (CBD) and Paris Climate Agreement finds constitutional foundation in Article 48-A. Recent National Green Tribunal decisions (2022-2024) consistently invoke the 42nd Amendment as constitutional basis for environmental regulations, particularly in mining, water pollution, and air quality standards.The amendment's Preamble changes introduced 'socialist' and 'secular' concepts, which the Supreme Court in Kesavananda Bharati case interpretation held as basic structure elements. The environmental component, though less litigated than socioeconomic aspects, has become increasingly relevant post-2020 with climate change litigation.",
