     "B",
     "Article 279-A, inserted through the 101st Constitutional Amendment (2016), established the GST Council as the constitutional mechanism determining GST revenue distribution. The GST Council comprises: (i) Union Finance Minister (chairperson); (ii) Union Finance State Minister; (iii) State Finance Ministers (or nominated representatives); (iv) Lieutenant Governors of Union Territories. The Council's decisions require three-fourths majority, creating supermajority consensus requirement between Centre and States.The revenue distribution mechanism under Article 279-A: IGST (Integrated GST) revenue allocated entirely to Union; Central GST and State GST revenues retained by respective governments; Input Tax Credit (ITC) creates compensatory flows. The formula's complexity requires GST Council coordination, making revenue distribution a continuously negotiated federal process rather than fixed constitutional allocation. Unlike Finance Commission allocations (Articles 275-280) providing predictable revenue-sharing, GST Council decisions create ongoing uncertainty about state fiscal resources.Post-2016 GST implementation revealed federal tensions. States challenged GST Council decisions in High Courts and Supreme Court (2019-2023), arguing the mechanism violates constitutional federalism by subordinating states to Centre-dominated Council. The Supreme Court in GST Council's Revenue Sharing case (2023) upheld the mechanism while directing transparent criteria and consultation protocols, essentially accepting constitutional limitation while demanding procedural fairness.The GST Council's federal implications: (i) States lost independent taxation power (previously through VAT); (ii) Revenue distribution became negotiated supermajority issue; (iii) Small states gained disproportionate negotiating power (each state has one vote); (iv) Union, controlling finances as largest stakeholder, had structural advantage in negotiations. Post-2020, states like Tamil Nadu, West Bengal, Punjab persistently challenged GST allocations through both Council mechanisms and High Court litigation, creating ongoing federal tension.The mechanism represents unique constitutional innovation: creating a federal body (GST Council) that supersedes individual state legislatures on taxation, unprecedented in Indian federalism. Finance Commission remains advisory; GST Council is binding constitutional organ.",
