     "B",
     "Article 370 (now largely repealed through 2019 amendment) granted Jammu & Kashmir a quasi-federal status within the Indian Union, allowing the State its own constitution, autonomous governance, and differential application of Union laws. Article 35-A (derived from Article 370(1)(d)) granted J&K Legislative Assembly power to define state subjects and restrict non-permanent residents' property rights and employment — creating constitutional separatism. The 2019 amendment, reclassifying J&K as Union Territory with separate legislature, fundamentally altered this constitutional architecture.Article 371 series (371A-371J) extends special status to specific states: Nagaland (371A), Assam (371B), Manipur (371C), Andhra Pradesh (371D-E), Arunachal Pradesh (371F-I), Goa (371J). These articles allow tailored constitutional provisions for historically distinct regions: tribal autonomy, cultural protection, special property laws, separate governance structures. Unlike Article 370's broad autonomy framework, Articles 371-series provide targeted constitutional accommodations within unified Indian framework.The constitutional architecture reveals federal philosophy: while Union remains ultimate sovereign (Article 1: India is union of states), constitutional designers accommodated regional specificities through targeted amendments. Nagaland's Article 371A exempts religious and social laws (recognized tribal custom) from Parliament's legislative reach, creating constitutional safe space for indigenous governance. Assam's Article 371B allows Northeast states federation formation through President's order, enabling cooperative federalism.Post-2019, Article 370's repeal represents the most significant constitutional reconfiguration of Centre-state relations in recent history. The Supreme Court in multiple petitions (2019-2023) upheld the amendment's validity while noting the extraordinary nature of altering entrenched J&K status. Justice Chandrachud's 2023 order suggested that while amendments can alter special status (as occurred), they must follow constitutional procedure — implicitly establishing that J&K's prior status was constitutionally entrenched beyond ordinary amendment.The remaining Articles 371-series continue providing federal constitutional distinctiveness for Northeast and southern regions, representing the Constitution's accommodation of diversity within federal structure. These articles demonstrate that Indian federalism isn't uniform; it's asymmetric, allowing constitutional variation reflecting historical, cultural, and political differences.",
