     "Finance Commission's approval / ఆర్థిక సంఘం ఆమోదం",
     "B",
     "Article 262 grants Parliament exclusive power to legislate on inter-state water disputes and other inter-state disputes, requiring Parliament's consent for inter-state compacts. More broadly, Article 263 empowers the President to set up Inter-state Council for resolving inter-state disputes, but this doesn't itself authorize compacts; compacts require either Parliament legislation or explicit constitutional provision. The constitutional framework treats inter-state agreements as potentially affecting constitutional structure, requiring parliamentary oversight rather than executive authorization alone.The constitutional logic: Since federal systems allocate territorial authority among states, compacts modifying state boundaries, jurisdiction, or statutory powers must receive parliamentary confirmation to prevent states from unilaterally altering constitutional federalism. The USA's Constitution requires Congressional consent for interstate compacts (Article I, Section 10), reflecting federal principle that state compacts potentially affect national structure. India's Article 262 mirrors this approach for critical disputes.Notable inter-state compacts requiring parliamentary involvement: (i) Inter-state Water Sharing Agreements (Krishna, Godavari, Narmada river disputes) require Parliament legislation establishing statutory tribunals; (ii) Interstate commerce agreements; (iii) Border demarcation agreements. The Kaveri Waters Disputes Tribunal, established through legislation referencing Article 262, exemplifies parliamentary involvement in inter-state compacts. States cannot independently create water-sharing arrangements; constitutional mechanism requires parliamentary statute.Post-2020, inter-state disputes increased: (i) Power sharing disputes (2020-2023); (ii) Water allocation disputes intensifying with climate change; (iii) Environmental pollution disputes (Yamuna, Ganges pollution between states). These disputes invoke Article 262 as constitutional basis for Supreme Court jurisdiction and parliamentary legislative response. The constitutional framers anticipated such disputes by placing inter-state compact authority with Parliament rather than executive, preventing states from circumventing constitutional allocations through private agreements.Article 263's Inter-state Council remains advisory; it recommends dispute resolution but cannot authorize constitutional-modifying compacts. Parliament retains ultimate authority through Article 262 jurisdiction, representing constitutional safeguard against states undermining federalism through collusive agreements.",
